---
// The four layers of a Familiar's memory, from what is always in view down
// to the transcript everything else is derived from.
---

<figure class="diagram" aria-label="The layers of a Familiar's memory">
  <div class="diagram-label">Always in view</div>
  <div class="diagram-box accent">
    <strong>Memory section of the prompt</strong>
    <p>
      The profile of each person the Familiar works with, and a one-line roster
      of everyone and everything else it knows about.
    </p>
    <small>Bounded by the number of people, not by how long it has run.</small>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-arrow">profiles are written from notes</div>
  <div class="diagram-label">Kept and searched</div>
  <div class="diagram-row">
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <strong>Pages</strong>
      <p>
        One per person or thing. Notes: what is true of them now, each resting
        on a quoted message.
      </p>
      <small>Change by replacement.</small>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-box">
      <strong>Journal</strong>
      <p>
        Dated lines, one per thing worth finding again, each pointing at the
        exchange it came from.
      </p>
      <small>Only grows. One document per month.</small>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-arrow">notes and journal lines quote it</div>
  <div class="diagram-label">Ground truth</div>
  <div class="diagram-box">
    <strong>Transcript</strong>
    <p>
      The Familiar's whole conversation across every room and DM, exactly as it
      happened. Never copied, never summarised away.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="diagram-box quiet">
    <strong>Search (derived)</strong>
    <p>
      One word index over notes, journal lines and past exchanges, kept in
      memory and rebuilt from the layers above. It owns nothing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Each layer has one job. Only the top one reaches every request; the rest
    are found when they're needed.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
